The user's report that host_1_1_1_1 is locked out of all 10.200.0.x servers is not supported by any live evidence in the network.

**Affected component:** None identified. Every network layer was inspected and found healthy.

**Mechanism:** A complete Phase A/B/D diagnostic sweep was performed. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed that host_1_1_1_1 holds a valid IPv4 address, a correct default gateway, and the correct nameserver (10.200.0.2). No ACL drop rules, no static ARP overrides, no MAC conflicts, and no gateway/resolver divergence were found. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed that OSPF is fully converged across all six FRR routers, each carrying 9 routes, with all adjacencies in Full state. No traffic-control shaping or queuing anomalies exist on any device. Phase D (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) confirmed that host_1_1_1_1 successfully resolves all four web server hostnames to correct IPs and receives HTTP 200 from each server within 4 ms. No resource pressure, stress tools, or socket exhaustion were detected anywhere.

**Localization:** No device implicated.

**Root cause:** The reported connectivity failure is unconfirmed. The network is operating correctly at every layer — L2 addressing, L3 routing, DNS resolution, and HTTP service delivery all succeed from host_1_1_1_1 to the 10.200.0.x subnet. There is no flap script, no carrier transition history, and no daemon error that could explain an intermittent past outage. The symptom may have been transient and self-resolved before diagnostics ran, or may have been misattributed.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. Monitor if the user reports recurrence and capture a snapshot at failure time.
